<!--
    kuna option bejoin (off by default, GH-904; on here): SPARC returns a long long in %o0:%o1 with the
    HIGH word in %o0, and returns an int in %o0 alone.
    The same join-order bug as kuna-bejoin.xml (the PowerPC case): return
    recovery joined %o0 as the LOW word, so wide_mul printed
    CONCAT44(low,high).
        wide_mul:  return CONCAT44((int4)((int8)a1 * a0),... >> 0x20);   (halves swapped)
    SPARC's restore copies every in-register back to its out-register, so %o1
    reaches the RETURN holding whatever the function left in %i1 on every
    function that opened a window, and a function returning one int seems to
    return a pair.  Joined first register low, such a pair narrows back to %o0
    (the uncomputed-half repair, a byte return, C's truncation to bound's
    int4).  Joined in the ABI's order it kept %o1 instead: bound's pairs came
    back with their halves swapped, the byte readers returned char, and a
    function returning 0 returned the argument (return a1;).  So a pair is
    joined in the ABI's order only when %i1 holds a value the function put
    there to return; the entry value, scratch the function also used, and a
    literal zero keep the old join.  same, keep_zero, zero_after and
    one_after still print the argument shifted into the high word, as before:
    their int result folds into the pair before the repair can drop the
    leftover.
    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin on.
    Little-endian targets are byte-identical; the default (off) pass and the
    trade-off the option makes are in kuna-bejoin.xml.
    Source (clang 14 -fintegrated-as, sparc-linux-gnu):
        bejoin_sparc32_be.o (-O2) at 0x10000:
        long long wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
          save / smul %i1,%i0,%i1 / rd %y,%i0 / ret / restore
        unsigned int same(unsigned int a, unsigned int b) { return a == b; }
        int keep_zero(int *p, int b) { *p = b; return 0; }
        bejoin_window_sparc32_O2.o at 0x20000: back4, zero_after, one_after
        bejoin_window_sparc32_O0.o at 0x30000: mark
        bejoin_narrow_sparc32_O2.o at 0x40000 (the unrelocated call to ext
        prints as sub_*):
        unsigned bound(int *s, unsigned n) {
          unsigned a = n + (n >> 3) + 4, b = n + (n >> 5) + 7;
          ext();
          if (s[3]) return (a > b ? a : b) + 6;
          if (s[0] != 15) return (s[1] ? a : b) + s[2];
          return n + (n >> 12) + 13 + s[2];
        }
        int ibyte(unsigned char *p) { ext(); return *p; }
        unsigned ubyte(unsigned char *p) { ext(); return *p; }
        int ihalf(unsigned short *p) { ext(); return *p; }
        int ibyte_leaf(unsigned char *p) { return *p; }
    Assert #1: wide_mul is the signed 64-bit product, rd %y (the high word) in %o0.
    Asserts #2-#3: bound returns int4 and each of its pairs keeps the value in
    the low half, which the int4 return keeps.
    Asserts #4-#5: the byte and halfword readers return uint4, and back4 and
    mark one int in %o0.
    Assert #6: no function returns the second argument.
-->
